ources properly—the KB document and the RSC periodic table links for both iron and aluminum. I'm continuing to map out the remaining web sources to biblatex entries, assigning keys for the RSC aluminium page, the engineerfix article on dense metals, the LKAB minerals ballast resource, the NIST X-ray data, and the marshield gamma-ray attenuation page.\n\nNow I'm realizing the citekey format needs to follow specific rules—author and year for individuals, organization and year for institutions, with strict alphanumeric validation. For knowledge base files I'll use just the base name without a year, but for RSC entries I need to decide on a consistent approach since these online resources don't have traditional publication years. I'll treat RSC as an organization and use 2024 as the year to create valid keys like `RSC2024`, though I'm uncertain about the exact years for these web resources. I'm finishing up the citekey assignments for the remaining sources, then moving on to compile the slot outputs using the best available data from each source.\n\nFor slot-01, I'm pulling the melting point data from the knowledge base with proper citations showing iron at 1538°C, copper at 1085°C, and aluminum at 660°C, all ranked from highest to lowest. Now I'm working through slot-02 to identify which source provides the highest confidence data for copper's structural properties.\n\nLooking at the density comparison, copper comes in at 8.96 g/cm³, which is notably higher than iron's 7.874 g/cm³ and aluminum's 2.70 g/cm³. The functional significance here is that this density advantage makes copper valuable in applications requiring concentrated mass in tight spaces—counterweights in aircraft, ballast in ships, and radiation shielding all depend on this property to provide stability and attenuation.\n\nHowever, I'm realizing that density alone doesn't determine mechanical performance. Stiffness depends on elastic modulus rather than density, and wear resistance correlates more with hardness. Since the knowledge base only provides melting point and density for these metals, I'd need additional property data to make a complete comparison of their load-bearing or wear characteristics.\n\nNow I'm looking at how to best synthesize the available sources. The bullet-list format from one document is well-structured, while the flowing paragraphs from others provide richer context with citations. I should merge these approaches to create a concise comparison that draws on the specific data points—iron's melting point of 1538°C versus aluminum's 660°C, and their density difference where iron is roughly 2.9 times denser. I've decided the bullet list format works better for clarity and structure, so I'll use that approach with the citations from the knowledge base. Now I'm drafting the final canonical answers for each slot, starting with the metals ranked by melting point and their corresponding sources.\n\nFor the second slot, I'm explaining that copper has the highest density among the three metals, then describing how density functions as a critical property in engineering applications—from aircraft counterweights to radiation shielding—where concentrated mass in limited space is essential for performance. Iron significantly outpaces aluminum in thermal stability with a melting point nearly 880°C higher, while aluminum's lower density—roughly 2.9 times less—makes it advantageous for weight-sensitive applications. Iron occupies a higher position on the periodic table at atomic number 26 compared to aluminum's 13, and now I'm setting up the bibliographic references to support these comparisons.\n\nBuilding BibLaTeX entries... Continuing with the bibliography entries for the remaining sources on metal properties and industrial applications. Finishing up the Marshield citation and adding the Material Properties reference with their respective URLs and metadata.\n\nNow I'm setting the formatting rules—professional tone with LaTeX notation for numerical values,